    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "The array has complete rows of $5$ and an incomplete row. What division statement does it model?\n$\\begin{array}{ccccc}\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet\\\\\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet\\\\\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet\\\\\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\bullet&\\end{array}$",
        "solution_text": "There are $3$ complete rows of $5$ and $4$ counters in the incomplete row.\n$19=5\\times3+4$.\n$19\\div5=3$ remainder $4$.\nThe quotient counts the complete rows. The remainder $4$ is less than $5$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Share $23$ counters equally among $4$ groups. Describe the groups and the leftover counters.",
        "solution_text": "Put $5$ counters in each of the $4$ groups: $4\\times5=20$.\n$23-20=3$ counters remain.\nThere are $4$ equal groups of $5$, with $3$ counters left over.\n$23\\div4=5$ remainder $3$.\nHere the quotient counts the counters in each group, not the number of groups."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A model has $4$ complete rows of $6$ counters and an incomplete row of $5$. How many counters are there altogether?\n(b) Another model has $43$ counters in complete rows of $8$, with $3$ counters left over. How many complete rows are there?",
        "solution_text": "(a) $\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}6\\times4+5\\\\&=24+5\\\\&=29\\end{aligned}$ counters.\n(b) The complete rows contain $43-3=40$ counters.\n$40\\div8=5$.\nThere are $5$ complete rows of $8$, with $3$ left over.\nCheck: $8\\times5+3=43$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A student models $31\\div6$ with $4$ complete groups of $6$ and $7$ counters left over. Repair the model without changing the total.",
        "solution_text": "$7$ is not less than $6$, so another complete group can be made.\n$7=6+1$.\nMove $6$ of the leftovers into a fifth complete group.\n$31=6\\times5+1$.\nThe repaired model has $5$ complete groups and $1$ leftover. The total is still $31$, and $1<6$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A division model has fewer than $47$ counters, with $5$ full rows of $8$. What is the greatest possible non-zero remainder, and how many counters are shown?",
        "solution_text": "The full rows hold $8\\times5=40$ counters.\nA total below $47$ can be at most $46$.\n$46-40=6$.\nThe greatest possible remainder is $6$, giving $46$ counters. It is less than the row size $8$.\nA remainder of $7$ would give $47$ counters, which is not fewer than $47$."
      }
    ],
